---
// VISUAILS — one disclosure, used everywhere.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS
// Lucas, August 2026: "het wordt namelijk best druk om naar te kijken … bedenk
// manieren om dit tegen te gaan maar alsnog dezelfde info en stappen behouden."
//
// The site measured 2,435 words on the homepage across 24 sections, 1,212 in
// step 1 of the order flow, 1,567 on /ai-act with nothing collapsed at all —
// against a median of 346 across 41 pages. It is three places, not the site.
//
// Before this file there were FIVE different disclosures: .faq-item on
// /catalog, .acc-item on /faq, .hv-notfor, .hv-vat and .hv-faq-item on the
// homepage, and .pl-why in the order form. Every one is a native
// <details>/<summary> with the same plus-to-minus affordance and slightly
// different padding, which is five places to fix a focus ring and five chances
// for the sixth one to be invented. This is that component, once.
//
// THE RULE FOR WHEN A BLOCK EARNS ONE — written down in DESIGN.md too, because
// a component with no rule attached becomes a component used everywhere:
//
//   Collapse a block when it answers a question only SOME of your readers have.
//   Keep it open when every reader needs it to decide.
//
// And one hard line under that: NOTHING ON THE BELIEF LADDER IS EVER
// COLLAPSED. PRODUCT.md lists four rungs — it is not an AI toy, it holds up
// across a collection, it costs less and lands sooner, finding out costs €1
// — and those are what a sceptical visitor came to test. Hiding a rung behind a
// summary to win quiet is trading the argument for the aesthetic.
//
// NATIVE <details>, DELIBERATELY. It opens with JavaScript off, it is in the
// accessibility tree for free, ctrl+F in Chrome opens it to find a match, and
// it needs no state of ours. Everything below is presentation.
//
// `name` GIVES AN EXCLUSIVE ACCORDION WITH NO SCRIPT AT ALL. Two disclosures
// sharing a name close each other, in the browser, natively. That is what the
// order form's step 1 uses to show one picker at a time — see OrderFlow.astro.
// In a browser that does not support it they simply all open independently,
// which is the old behaviour and not a broken one.
//
// `summaryLive` is for the case step 1 needs: a summary that states the CURRENT
// ANSWER rather than a fixed label, written by pipeline.js. It renders a span
// with the given data attribute, so this component still holds no logic.
interface Props {
  /** The always-visible line. */
  summary: string;
  /** Open on load. For the one disclosure on a page that is the exception. */
  open?: boolean;
  /** Shared name → native exclusive accordion. */
  name?: string;
  /** data-attribute on a trailing span in the summary, for live answer text. */
  liveAttr?: string;
  /** Its starting text, so the summary reads correctly with no JS. */
  liveText?: string;
  /** 'panel' (bordered, for lists) | 'plain' (rule-only, for prose sections). */
  tone?: 'panel' | 'plain';
  class?: string;
}
const {
  summary, open = false, name, liveAttr, liveText = '', tone = 'panel', class: extra = '',
} = Astro.props;
---

<details
  class:list={['dc', `dc-${tone}`, extra]}
  open={open}
  name={name}
>
  <summary class="dc-sum">
    <span class="dc-label">{summary}</span>
    {liveAttr && <span class="dc-live" {...{ [liveAttr]: '' }}>{liveText}</span>}
  </summary>
  <div class="dc-body">
    <slot />
  </div>
</details>

<style>
  /* The marker is removed in three ways because the three engines disagree:
     list-style for Firefox, ::marker for the spec, ::-webkit-details-marker for
     older WebKit. Missing any one of them ships a stray triangle beside the
     glyph below. */
  .dc > .dc-sum { list-style: none; cursor: pointer; }
  .dc > .dc-sum::marker { content: ''; }
  .dc > .dc-sum::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }

  .dc-sum {
    /* Was 0px — the last survivor of the hard-edge era, and the only control on
       the site with no corner at all. Not a pill: this is a full-width row, and
       999px on something 700px wide draws two semicircles, not a button. It
       takes the field step, which is what a wide pressable row gets. */
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: .75rem;
    /* 24px is de ondergrens van WCAG 2.5.8 (AA) voor een raakvlak. Deze rij
       kwam op 23,5px uit — net eronder, en juist net eronder is het soort ding
       dat niemand ooit ziet zonder te meten. Een minimum en geen opvulling: de
       rij is bijna altijd al hoger, dus dit verandert niets aan hoe het eruit
       ziet en vangt alleen het geval van één korte regel op. */
    min-height: 24px;
    /* The glyph is a grid column rather than a float: a floated ::after drops
       out of flow, so a two-line summary wraps UNDER it and the plus ends up
       beside the first line with the second line running past it. */
    padding-right: 1.6rem;
    position: relative;
  }
  .dc-label { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); font-size: .98rem; line-height: 1.4; }
  /* The current answer, when there is one. Lighter than the label because it is
     the value and not the question, and it must never out-weigh the thing it
     answers to. */
  .dc-live { margin-left: auto; font-size: .86rem; color: var(--ink-3); text-align: right; }
  .dc-live:empty { display: none; }

  /* Plus to minus. A rotating chevron would be the reflex; the arithmetic
     glyphs are what every other disclosure on this site already used, so this
     preserves the existing vocabulary rather than introducing a sixth one. */
  .dc-sum::after {
    content: '+';
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    top: -0.1em;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1;
    transition: color var(--dur-1) var(--ease);
  }
  .dc[open] > .dc-sum::after { content: '\2212'; }
  .dc-sum:hover::after { color: var(--accent-bright); }

  .dc-sum:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--focus-ring); outline-offset: 3px; border-radius: var(--r-sm); }

  .dc-body { margin-top: .85rem; }
  /* Lucht onder een opengeklapt antwoord (4 september 2026): de laatste alinea
     stond op 0–2px van de rand van het volgende paneel. */
  .dc[open] > .dc-body { padding-bottom: .6rem; }
  /* Set on the body rather than on the component, so a caller can put anything
     inside — a list, a figure, a fieldset — without inheriting prose rules it
     did not ask for. */
  .dc-body :global(p) { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: .92rem; line-height: 1.65; }
  .dc-body :global(p + p) { margin-top: .7rem; }

  /* PANEL — bordered, for a stack of short answers. What /faq and the
     homepage's FAQ block already looked like. */
  /* PANEL — a stack of separate rows, not one bordered table. August 2026.
     Lucas: *"bij sommige elementen zit een soort grijze rand eromheen, maak
     dit mooier"*, with the FAQ stack as the example.

     What it was: `border: 1px solid var(--line)` with square corners, and
     `border-top: 0` on every panel after the first so eight questions read as
     one outlined box divided into eight cells. Two problems, and the second is
     the one that matters. The outline is dated — a grey rectangle on a
     near-black page is a 2009 table. But the SHARED border also said these
     eight things are one thing, when they are eight independent questions a
     visitor opens one at a time.

     So: real rows, with real space between them, each on the raised surface
     with its own radius and a lit top edge. The gap is small (6px) because
     they are still a list and not a grid of cards.

     NO .lift HERE, and that is deliberate against the global rule. A 1.2%
     scale on a 740px-wide row is nine pixels of growth, and with eight rows
     stacked the pointer walking down the list makes the whole column breathe.
     Lift is for objects with two dimensions; this is a line. It gets a surface
     change on hover instead, which is what a list row has always got. */
  .dc-panel {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    padding: 1.1rem 1.3rem;
    box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgb(255 255 255 / 0.05);
    transition: background var(--dur-1) var(--ease);
  }
  .dc-panel + .dc-panel { margin-top: 6px; }
  .dc-panel:hover, .dc-panel[open] { background: var(--surface-2); }

  /* PLAIN — a hairline rule and nothing else, for collapsing whole prose
     sections on a document page. A bordered box around four paragraphs of
     legal text would be a card, and cards are the lazy answer; the rule keeps
     the page reading as a document that happens to fold. */
  .dc-plain {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: 1.1rem 0;
  }
  .dc-plain + .dc-plain { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .dc-plain:last-of-type { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .dc-plain .dc-label { font-size: 1.05rem; }

  /* No height animation. A <details> cannot transition its own open state
     without scripting it, and the half-measures (animating the body's opacity
     after the fact) produce a flash on a slow paint. Instant is honest and is
     what every native accordion does. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .dc-sum::after { transition: none; }
  }
</style>
